WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.205
Lesson 10.2.

00:00:02.205 --> 00:00:04.185
Cash-and-carry basis trades.

00:00:04.185 --> 00:00:06.561
By the end of this lesson, you'll be able

00:00:06.561 --> 00:00:09.163
to capture the premium of a dated future over

00:00:09.163 --> 00:00:11.199
spot without taking a view on price.

00:00:12.499 --> 00:00:13.789
Here's the plan.

00:00:13.789 --> 00:00:15.474
First, the key ideas.

00:00:15.474 --> 00:00:18.189
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:18.189 --> 00:00:19.714
Then, your checklist.

00:00:19.714 --> 00:00:21.876
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:23.176 --> 00:00:27.256
In rising markets, dated futures usually trade above spot.

00:00:27.256 --> 00:00:29.592
The gap (basis) shrinks to zero

00:00:29.592 --> 00:00:32.521
at expiry because the future settles

00:00:32.521 --> 00:00:35.021
at spot. Buy spot + short the

00:00:35.021 --> 00:00:37.687
future at the same size and you

00:00:37.687 --> 00:00:41.236
lock that gap, whatever the price does,

00:00:41.236 --> 00:00:43.986
as long as both legs survive to

00:00:43.986 --> 00:00:47.595
expiry. annualised basis = (future ÷ spot

00:00:47.595 --> 00:00:49.056
− 1) × 365 ÷ days

00:00:50.356 --> 00:00:52.871
Let's work an example,

00:00:52.871 --> 00:00:57.621
step by step. ETH spot 3,000, 90-day future 3,060.

00:00:57.621 --> 00:01:01.206
minus 1,440 per ETH. Without enough margin

00:01:01.206 --> 00:01:04.462
on the futures venue, it's closed

00:01:04.462 --> 00:01:07.061
out and you're left long

00:01:07.061 --> 00:01:10.282
spot at the top. At expiry, if

00:01:10.282 --> 00:01:14.023
ETH is 2,000: spot leg minus 1,000,

00:01:14.023 --> 00:01:17.025
short future +1,060 to +60

00:01:17.025 --> 00:01:20.648
per ETH. At expiry, if ETH is 4,500:

00:01:20.648 --> 00:01:25.055
spot leg +1,500, short future minus 1,440 to

00:01:25.055 --> 00:01:29.272
+60 per ETH. The catch: before expiry, a

00:01:29.272 --> 00:01:32.519
rally to 4,500 means the short

00:01:32.519 --> 00:01:35.332
leg shows. You can check

00:01:35.332 --> 00:01:38.327
every number with the program's calculator.

00:01:39.627 --> 00:01:41.979
Margin and venue risk: a squeeze on

00:01:41.979 --> 00:01:44.332
the short leg, or the venue failing.

00:01:45.632 --> 00:01:47.092
Here's your checklist.

00:01:47.092 --> 00:01:48.627
Do it now, for real.

00:01:48.627 --> 00:01:51.708
First, Both legs opened together, same size.

00:01:51.708 --> 00:01:55.491
Next, Futures margin survives at least a +100%

00:01:55.491 --> 00:01:57.883
move, or a rule to top up.

00:01:57.883 --> 00:02:00.768
Then, Venue risk sized (cap per venue).

00:02:00.768 --> 00:02:03.595
And finally, Plan to hold to expiry; early

00:02:03.595 --> 00:02:05.502
exit may be at a worse basis.

00:02:06.802 --> 00:02:08.012
Question 1.

00:02:08.012 --> 00:02:12.372
Spot 2,000, 180-day future 2,080.

00:02:12.372 --> 00:02:13.657
Annualised basis?

00:02:18.077 --> 00:02:24.285
The answer: 4% × 365/180 ≈ 8.1%.

00:02:25.585 --> 00:02:26.830
Question 2.

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:29.325
Why is the return "locked" at expiry?

00:02:33.745 --> 00:02:36.394
The answer: The future settles at spot, so

00:02:36.394 --> 00:02:38.674
the gain on one leg offsets the loss

00:02:38.674 --> 00:02:41.015
on the other, leaving the entry basis.

00:02:42.315 --> 00:02:43.585
Question 3.

00:02:43.585 --> 00:02:45.500
What breaks the trade before expiry?

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:53.009
The answer: A margin call or liquidation on the

00:02:53.009 --> 00:02:55.970
short future during a rally, or venue failure.

00:02:57.270 --> 00:02:59.785
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:02:59.785 --> 00:03:04.365
Next up, Lesson 10.3: Delta-neutral funding carry, done properly.
